ects rooks indirectly and supports e4 or prepares queenside castling, but less urgent than development or central control.\n- **B: c1-g5** or **B: c1-e3** are possible, but c1-bishop is blocked after b2-b3 or similar, and immediate development may be slow.\n\nHowever, note that **e4xd5** is not just a regular capture — since Black\'s pawn moved two squares to d5 last turn, and White\'s pawn is on e4, **exd5 (e4xd5)** is an en passant capture, removing the d5 pawn and landing on d5. This is a key tactical opportunity.\n\nBut wait — en passant must be played immediately after the two-square advance. Since Black just played ...d7-d5, and it\'s now White’s turn, en passant is legal.\n\nThus, **P: e4-d5** is likely **intended to represent the en passant capture**, even though it\'s not labeled "ep" — in many systems, it\'s just written as e4xd5.\n\nAlso, capturing on d5 eliminates Black\'s central pawn presence and opens lines for White’s pieces.\n\nReason:  \nThe best move is **P: e4-d5**, the en passant capture, because it:\n- Removes Black’s central pawn,\n- Opens the e4 square for future pieces,\n- Gains space,\n- Exploits a tactical rule (en passant) that must be used immediately,\n- Prevents Black from maintaining a strong pawn center.\n\nThis move maximizes activity and initiative early in the game.\n\nAnswer: P: e4-d5'}
